## Observation 23: Altered Coordination During Conscious Opposing Limb Movement

Time: 16:25 HST
Date: 2026-09-20
Location: Honolulu, Oahu, Hawaii

### Context

This observation occurred during continued exclusive mouth inhalation and exhalation while walking.

Attention was directed toward the mechanics of arm movement and coordination during normal walking.

### Observation

While walking, conscious attention was applied to the movement of the arms and the timing of limb coordination.

Coordination or movements seem to be processed abd initiated by areas of unconscious concentration like the knees. 

### Description

- Normally, arm swinging appears to occur automatically during walking.
- Mental concentration was directed toward the movement pattern.
- While concentrating, an intentional instruction was given to perform a movement that opposed the body's normal walking pattern.
- Specifically, attention was directed toward moving one limb in a manner that differed from the automatically coordinated movement occurring during gait.
- When attempting to consciously override the normal pattern, an unusual sensation or internal mismatch was perceived.
- The body appeared to react differently when the consciously directed movement conflicted with the automatic movement pattern.
- Physical response or stimulation can be directed to certain areas.
### Findings

- Conscious involvement altered the experience of movement coordination.
- The automatic movement pattern became more noticeable once attention was focused on it.
- A clear difference was perceived between automatic movement and intentionally directed movement.
- Attempting to perform an opposing movement pattern produced a noticeable internal response or disruption in coordination.

### Learning

Movement patterns that normally operate automatically may become more noticeable when conscious attention is directed toward modifying or overriding them.

### Interpretation

This observation documents a subjective difference between:

- Automatic coordination during walking.
- Intentionally directed coordination during walking.

The observation suggests that conscious intervention may alter the perception of movement patterns that typically operate without deliberate control.

